 <div class="bg-amber-50 border-l-4 border-amber-500 rounded-r-xl p-6 mb-8 speakable">
 <p class="text-[11px] font-black text-brandOrange uppercase tracking-widest mb-2">KEY TAKEAWAYS</p>
 <p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed text-sm mb-4">Before you pay the balance on your PO, verify every certification document the factory sent you. These 5 database checks take under 10 minutes and catch the most common fraud patterns: photoshopped logos, expired certificates, and labs that don't exist. The FCC ban on Chinese testing labs (May 2026) makes verification even more critical — any new FCC certificate claiming a China-based lab is automatically invalid.</p>
 <ul class="text-sm text-slate-700 space-y-2 list-disc pl-5">
 <li><strong>FCC ID lookup (fccid.io or FCC OET):</strong> Enter the Grantee Code + Product Code from the label. If the ID returns no results, doesn't match your product, or shows a China lab post-May 2026 — the certificate is invalid. Budget $500-1,200 for FCC SDoC, $2,000-5,000 for FCC ID (wireless).</li>
 <li><strong>UL File Number verification (UL Product iQ):</strong> UL certificates reference a File Number (e.g., E123456). Enter it at productiq.ul.com. A valid result shows the exact model numbers covered. If the file lists a different product or the number doesn't exist, the factory is misrepresenting its UL status.</li>
 <li><strong>CE Declaration of Conformity check:</strong> The DoC must be in YOUR company name, not the factory's. Customs authorities reject factory-signed DoCs. The notified body number (4 digits) on the certificate must match an active EU NANDO database listing.</li>
 <li><strong>GS Mark database lookup (Certipedia):</strong> GS (<em>Geprüfte Sicherheit</em>) certificates require annual factory audits. Search certipedia.com for the certificate ID. A missing or expired entry means the factory hasn't passed a recent audit — even if the logo on your document looks real.</li>
 <li><strong>Visual red flags:</strong> Blurry logos, missing test report reference numbers, certificates that list "all models" instead of your specific SKU, and test dates more than 5 years old. Request original test reports — not just certificate front pages — and archive them for customs clearance and retailer onboarding.</li>
 </ul>
 </div>

