 <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-brandBlue uppercase italic mb-6">8. Who Is Legally Responsible? Importer Obligations</h2>
 <p class="text-slate-600 mb-4">In both the US and EU, <strong>the importer, not the overseas factory, bears legal responsibility</strong> for compliance. US Customs and Border Protection (CBP) seizes non-compliant electronics at ports, in 2025, CBP flagged over $120 million in uncertified consumer electronics for destruction or re-export. In the EU, national market surveillance authorities can force product recalls and impose <strong>fines up to 4% of annual turnover</strong> under the Market Surveillance Regulation (EU 2019/1020). This makes <a href="/blog/how-to-choose-factory" class="text-brandBlue hover:text-brandOrange font-semibold">factory selection and verification</a> the single most important step in your certification strategy, a factory that swaps components after certifying puts your brand directly in the path of CBP seizure or EU recall orders.</p>
 <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 mb-4">
 <div class="bg-slate-50 rounded-xl p-6 border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
 <h4 class="font-black text-brandBlue uppercase mb-3">US Importer Obligations</h3>
 <ul class="text-slate-600 text-sm space-y-2">
 <li>Ensure FCC authorization before marketing</li>
 <li>FCC can seize non-compliant devices at customs</li>
 <li>Amazon removes listings without proof</li>
 <li>Maintain compliance records</li>
 </ul>
 </div>
 <div class="bg-slate-50 rounded-xl p-6 border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
 <h4 class="font-black text-brandBlue uppercase mb-3">EU Importer Obligations</h3>
 <ul class="text-slate-600 text-sm space-y-2">
 <li>Appoint EU authorized representative</li>
 <li>Ensure CE Technical File is in place</li>
 <li>Verify production maintains conformity</li>
 <li>Maintain docs for 10 years</li>
 </ul>
 </div>
 </div>
 </div>
 </section>

 <!-- Section 9: How to Verify Certifications Are Real -->
 <section id="verify" class="mb-16">
 <div class="bg-slate-50 rounded-xl p-6 border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
 <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-brandBlue uppercase italic mb-6">9. How to Verify Charger Certifications Are Real</h2>

 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">A product marked "CE" or "FCC" doesn't automatically mean it's certified. According to EU market surveillance data, <strong>44% of electrical products checked were non-compliant</strong> despite bearing conformity marks. Before you import, you need to verify that your supplier's certifications are genuine — not just a printed logo on the label.</p>

 <h3 class="font-black text-slate-900 uppercase mb-3">1. FCC ID Lookup (US Market)</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Every FCC-certified device has a unique <strong>FCC ID</strong> printed on the product label. Verify it in 60 seconds:</p>
 <ol class="list-decimal pl-5 text-slate-600 text-sm space-y-2 mb-4">
 <li>Find the FCC ID on the charger label (format: <strong>2 letters + numbers</strong>, e.g. 2AOKL-WOC42)</li>
 <li>Go to <a href="https://www.fcc.gov/oet/ea/fccid" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-brandBlue hover:text-brandOrange">fcc.gov/oet/ea/fccid</a></li>
 <li>Enter the FCC ID — if genuine, you'll see the manufacturer name, test reports, and grant date</li>
 <li><strong>Red flag:</strong> No result, or manufacturer name doesn't match your supplier</li>
 </ol>

 <h3 class="font-black text-slate-900 uppercase mb-3">2. UL File Number Verification</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">UL certification to <strong>UL 62368-1</strong> is verified through UL's public database:</p>
 <ol class="list-decimal pl-5 text-slate-600 text-sm space-y-2 mb-4">
 <li>Look for the UL mark with a file number (format: <strong>E + 6 digits</strong>, e.g. E123456)</li>
 <li>Search at <a href="https://productiq.ul.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-brandBlue hover:text-brandOrange">productiq.ul.com</a></li>
 <li>Verify the file number matches your supplier's legal entity name</li>
 <li><strong>Red flag:</strong> File number belongs to a different company, or certificate is expired</li>
 </ol>

 <h3 class="font-black text-slate-900 uppercase mb-3">3. CE Marking &amp; EU Declaration of Conformity</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">The CE mark is a <strong>self-declaration</strong> — the manufacturer prints it themselves. This makes it the most commonly counterfeited mark. Three-step verification:</p>
 <ul class="list-disc pl-5 text-slate-600 text-sm space-y-2 mb-4">
 <li><strong>Request the DoC:</strong> Every CE-marked product must have a signed EU Declaration of Conformity listing the specific directives (LVD 2014/35/EU, EMC 2014/30/EU, RED 2014/53/EU for wireless) and harmonized standards applied (EN 62368-1, EN 55032, EN 55035)</li>
 <li><strong>Check the Notified Body number:</strong> If the CE mark includes a 4-digit number (e.g. CE 0197), verify it at <a href="https://ec.europa.eu/growth/tools-databases/nando/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-brandBlue hover:text-brandOrange">EU NANDO database</a>. Fake numbers are common — the database confirms if the body is real</li>
 <li><strong>Cross-check the test report:</strong> The DoC must reference a test report from an accredited lab. Ask for the original PDF with the lab's letterhead — not a photocopy</li>
 </ul>

 <h3 class="font-black text-slate-900 uppercase mb-3">4. GS Mark (Germany) — The Gold Standard</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">GS (<em>Geprüfte Sicherheit</em>) is voluntary but the most trusted EU safety mark because it requires <strong>factory inspection</strong>, not just product testing.</p>
 <ul class="list-disc pl-5 text-slate-600 text-sm space-y-2 mb-4">
 <li>Verify at issuing body's certificate database (TÜV Rheinland: <a href="https://www.certipedia.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-brandBlue hover:text-brandOrange">certipedia.com</a>, TÜV SÜD, SGS, DEKRA, VDE)</li>
 <li>GS mark guarantees <strong>annual factory audits</strong> — a CE mark alone does not</li>
 </ul>

 <h3 class="font-black text-slate-900 uppercase mb-3">5. Quick Visual Red Flags</h3>
 <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-4">
 <table class="w-full text-sm text-left border-collapse">
 <thead><tr class="bg-slate-200"><th class="p-3 font-black text-slate-700 uppercase text-xs">What You See</th><th class="p-3 font-black text-slate-700 uppercase text-xs">What It Means</th></tr></thead>
 <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200">
 <tr><td class="p-3 text-slate-600">"CE" in wrong font (curved C, flat E)</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600">Fake. Real CE uses specific proportions: semicircle C + E with horizontal bars</td></tr>
 <tr><td class="p-3 text-slate-600">"China Export" mark (narrow spacing)</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600">Not the same as CE — deliberate counterfeit. Arms almost touching = China Export</td></tr>
 <tr><td class="p-3 text-slate-600">FCC logo without FCC ID number</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600">Invalid. FCC Part 15 requires FCC ID on the product, not just the logo</td></tr>
 <tr><td class="p-3 text-slate-600">UL mark without file number</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600">Likely fake. All UL-listed products must show the UL file number</td></tr>
 <tr><td class="p-3 text-slate-600">CE + FCC + UL all on same label, no numbers</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600">High probability of counterfeit — genuine multi-cert products have traceable IDs</td></tr>
 </tbody>
 </table>
 </div>

 <div class="bg-brandBlue/5 border-l-4 border-brandOrange p-4 rounded-r-xl mb-4">
 <p class="text-slate-700 text-sm"><strong>Bottom line:</strong> Never accept a certification at face value. A printed mark costs nothing to add. What matters is the <strong>verifiable paper trail</strong> — FCC ID, UL file number, EU Declaration of Conformity, and test reports from accredited labs. <strong>WOWOHCOOL provides all certification documents with OEM orders</strong>, including original test reports, DoC, and database lookup instructions for every certification on your product.</p>
 </div>
 </div>
 </section>

 <!-- Section 10: Pitfalls -->
 <section id="pitfalls" class="mb-16">
 <div class="bg-slate-50 rounded-xl p-6 border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
 <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-brandBlue uppercase italic mb-6">10. Common Compliance Pitfalls &amp; How to Avoid Them</h2>
 <div class="space-y-4">
 <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-200 rounded-xl p-5"><h4 class="font-black text-red-700 uppercase mb-2">Assuming CE = FCC</h3><p class="text-slate-600 text-sm">No mutual recognition. Separate testing always required.</p></div>
 <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-xl p-5"><h4 class="font-black text-amber-700 uppercase mb-2">Fake Factory Certificates</h3><p class="text-slate-600 text-sm">Always verify against issuer database (FCC ID, UL Product iQ).</p></div>
 <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-200 rounded-xl p-5"><h4 class="font-black text-red-700 uppercase mb-2">Component Substitutions</h3><p class="text-slate-600 text-sm">Factories may swap certified components after certification. Prevent this with <a href="/blog/quality-control-guide" class="text-brandBlue hover:text-brandOrange font-semibold">pre-shipment quality control inspections</a> verifying BOM against certification submittal.</p></div>
 <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-xl p-5"><h4 class="font-black text-amber-700 uppercase mb-2">Missing UL Annual Audit</h3><p class="text-slate-600 text-sm">Loss of listing = loss of Amazon and retail access.</p></div>
 <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-200 rounded-xl p-5"><h4 class="font-black text-red-700 uppercase mb-2">No EU Authorized Representative</h3><p class="text-slate-600 text-sm">Required by EU law. Products can be blocked at customs.</p></div>
 <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-xl p-5"><h4 class="font-black text-amber-700 uppercase mb-2">USB-C PD Handshake Failure</h3><p class="text-slate-600 text-sm">Having a Type-C port is not enough. PD protocol must pass compliance testing.</p></div>
 </div>
 </div>
 </section>

