 <!-- FAQ -->
 <section id="faq" class="mb-16">
 <div class="bg-slate-50 rounded-2xl p-8 border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
 <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-brandBlue uppercase italic mb-8 text-center">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
 <div class="space-y-6 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6"><h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">What certifications do OEM importers need to sell chargers in the US?</h3><p class="text-slate-600 text-sm faq-answer">FCC Part 15B mandatory (SDoC for wired, FCC ID for wireless). UL 62368-1 is commercially essential, Amazon and major retailers require UL listing or face delisting. ETL/CSA are accepted NRTL alternatives. Budget $1,500-3,000 for UL, $500-1,200 for FCC SDoC. Timeline: 6-10 weeks for full US certification. DOE Level VI energy efficiency compliance is also mandatory for external power supplies.</p></div>
 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6"><h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">What certifications are required for EU charger sales?</h3><p class="text-slate-600 text-sm faq-answer">CE marking covering LVD (EN 62368-1), EMC Directive (EN 55032/55035), and RoHS. EU Common Charger Directive 2022/2380 mandates USB-C for all chargers. EcoDesign 2025/2052: standby ≤0.1W, active efficiency ≥87%. WEEE registration required. The importer must sign the Declaration of Conformity in their own company name, a factory's DoC is legally invalid. Budget $1,500-3,500 for full CE compliance.</p></div>
 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6"><h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">What is the difference between UL, CE, FCC, and RoHS?</h3><p class="text-slate-600 text-sm faq-answer">CE (EU) covers safety + EMC + RoHS + energy efficiency, comprehensive conformity. FCC (US) covers electromagnetic interference only, does NOT address electrical safety. UL fills the US safety gap commercially and is required by major retailers. RoHS restricts hazardous substances and is mandatory for both EU and US markets. CE uses self-declaration with Technical File; FCC requires testing by an accredited lab submitted to the FCC; UL requires NRTL testing plus quarterly factory follow-up inspections.</p></div>
 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6"><h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">How long does certification take and how much does it cost?</h3><p class="text-slate-600 text-sm faq-answer">FCC: 2-4 weeks, $500-1,200. CE: 3-6 weeks, $1,500-3,500. UL: 6-10 weeks, $1,500-3,000 plus $1,500-3,000 annual factory audit. Full US+EU certification package: $3,000-8,000 per SKU. Using pre-certified ODM platforms reduces timeline to 3-4 weeks. CE/FCC/RoHS package with UN38.3 included: $2,000-4,000. Always budget for pre-compliance testing ($500-2,000) before full certification to catch issues early.</p></div>
 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6"><h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">Can OEM buyers use a pre-certified ODM platform instead of certifying from scratch?</h3><p class="text-slate-600 text-sm faq-answer">Yes. Many ODM manufacturers offer charger platforms that already carry UL, CE, and FCC certifications. OEM buyers can customize branding, connectors, cables, and packaging while leveraging the ODM's pre-approved certification work. This reduces certification timeline from 8-16 weeks to 3-4 weeks and cuts costs by 50-70%. Always verify the certification matches your exact model and market, a generic "series certificate" that doesn't cover your SKU is worthless. WOWOHCOOL offers pre-certified ODM platforms across all charger categories with full documentation.</p></div>
 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6"><h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">Can Chinese labs still perform FCC certification testing in 2026?</h3><p class="text-slate-600 text-sm faq-answer">No. In May 2026, the FCC voted to prohibit all China-based testing labs from equipment authorization for US-bound electronics. All new FCC certifications must use US/EU-based FCC-approved labs (TUV, Intertek, UL, SGS, MET Labs). This adds 1-2 weeks for international sample shipping and may increase testing costs 15-30%. Existing FCC certifications from Chinese labs remain valid and grandfathered. Verify current lab status at apps.fcc.gov before submitting any new equipment authorization.</p></div>
 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6"><h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">Who is legally responsible for certification, the factory or the importer?</h3><p class="text-slate-600 text-sm faq-answer">The importer bears full legal responsibility. The Declaration of Conformity must be in your company name. Your company name and address must appear on packaging as the responsible party. Never accept a factory-provided CE certificate with their name as the declaration holder, customs authorities will reject it. Maintain a technical file with original test reports for at least 10 years per EU requirements. <strong>WOWOHCOOL provides complete certification documentation with every OEM order, including original test reports, DoC templates, and compliance binders for US and EU markets. <a href="/contact/" class="text-brandBlue hover:text-brandOrange underline">Request certification support for your product line.</a></strong></p></div>
 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6"><h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">What should OEM importers check before shipping their first order?</h3><p class="text-slate-600 text-sm faq-answer">Five-point pre-shipment checklist: (1) Verify certifications match your exact SKU and target markets, not a generic series certificate. (2) Confirm hardware matches the certified unit exactly, same PCB, components, and firmware. (3) Get FCC ID or SDoC documentation plus UL file numbers, and cross-check them on official databases. (4) Lock the BOM in your purchase agreement to prevent unauthorized component substitutions that invalidate certifications. (5) Request and archive original test reports, not just certificate front pages, as these are required for customs clearance and retailer onboarding.</p></div>
 </div>
 </div>
 </section>

